Abstract
A biodegradable cellulose-based air filter (Ag-MOFs@CNF@ZIF-8) with multilayer structure was fabricated by in situ generation of double-component metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and reinforcement of cellulose nanofiber (CNF). It exhibits good filtration performance, gas adsorption, antibacterial activity and mechanical property. The presence of MOFs could enhance the interaction between the filter and particulate matter (PM) and significantly improve the specific surface area of the composite filter. Thus, the filtration efficiency of the composite filter could reach $94.3\%$ for $\mathrm{PM}_{2.5}$ and the nitrogen adsorption capacity increased to $109\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, the AgMOFs@CNF@ZIF-8 filter exhibited excellent antibacterial activity against Escherichia coli with an inhibition zone diameter of $18.1\mathrm{\mm}$ . The compressive strength of the composite filter could be up to $501\mathrm{kPa}$ , approximately 3.8 times higher than that of pure cellulose filter. Herein, this composite filter has a great application potential in PM2.5 removal, toxic gas adsorption and healthcare fields.
Keywords: Cellulose filter, metal organic frameworks, cellulose nanofiber, PM2.5
capture, antibacterial activity
## 1. Introduction
With rapid urbanization and industrialization, air pollution, especially PM2.5 (aerodynamic diameter $\leq2.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ ) (Liang, Duan, He, & Ma, 2016) and toxic gases, has caused a severe impact on human health and daily activities (Petäjä et al., 2016), as well as influencing environment and climate. However, most of commercial filters (Wan et al., 2014; Zuo et al., 2017) are fabricated from non-degradable petroleum-based materials. And its disposal would bring secondary pollution and limit further application. Among green raw materials, cellulose as one of the most abundant natural polymers has been regarded as a promising candidate to replace petroleum-based polymers for fabricating biodegradable air filters due to its excellent adsorption ability, chemical resistance and flexibility (Souzandeh, Wang, & Zhong, 2016). Although numerous researchers (Mao, Grgic, Finlay, Kadla, & Kerekes, 2008; Ma, Zhang, Yang, Song, & Nie, 2018) have prepared cellulose-based filters for efficiently removing particulate matter (PM). Some limitations of traditional cellulose-based filters still exist, such as lacking toxic gases adsorption ability and vulnerability to bacterial infection (Yoon, Kim, Ahn, & Ko, 2016). Therefore, there are urgent needs and great challenges to design and fabricate multifunctional air filters.
To endow cellulose-based filters with functionalities, various materials including zeolite, metal oxides and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) were employed to prepare filters (Purdue & Qiao, 2018; Chauhan & Mohanty, 2015; Chen et al., 2017). Among these materials, MOFs have been widely used in antibacterial fields (Lu, et al., 2014) and gas adsorption, separation aspects (Matsumoto & Kitaoka, 2016), due to large specific surface area and versatile functionalities. Especially, zeolitic imidazolate frameworks-8 (ZIF-8) (Neelakanda, Barankova, & Peinemann, 2016) and Ag-based metal–organic frameworks (Ag-MOFs) $[\mathrm{Ag}_{2}$ (HBTC)(im)] (Lu, Ye, Zhang, et al., 2014) were commonly used to prepare antibacterial and adsorbent materials. Russell et al. reported that ZIF-8 crystals exhibited excellent adsorption capacity for nitrogen $(\Nu_{2})$ , carbon monoxide (CO) and methane (CH4) due to a gate-opening transition (Russell, & Migone, 2017). Moreover, many researchers have fabricated cellulose@MOFs composites (Zhu et al., 2018; Ren et al., 2018) applied in gas adsorption (Ma et al., 2018) and antibacterial fields (Zhu, Yang, Cranston, & Zhu, 2016). However, organic solvents such as N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) are commonly used as reaction media to synthesize MOFs composites as mentioned above, which is harm to human health because of its high toxicity and carcinogenicity. Therefore, it is necessary to employ double-component systems including ZIF-8 and Ag-MOFs based on a DMF-free green process for explore and prepare multifunctional cellulose-based air filters.
In addition, mechanical strength is also a key factor for commercial air filters. Cellulose nanofiber (CNF) has drawn considerable interest as a green reinforcing agent due to high strength and modulus, abundant active groups and good biocompatibility (Sehaqui, Zhou, & Berglund, 2011). Qian and coworkers reported that the mechanical property of cellulose-based composites was significantly improved by interweaving with CNF (Qian et al., 2018). Svagan et al. employed native cellulose nanofibrils to reinforce the pore wall structure of foam materials (Svagan, Samir, & Berglund, 2008). These results indicated that CNF was a suitable bio-based reinforcing agent to strengthen the mechanical property of filters.
In this study, a biodegradable and multifunctional Ag-MOFs $@$ CNF@ZIF-8 air filter with multi-layer structure was prepared by in situ green deposition method. Cellulose fibers were firstly functionalized by ZIF-8 and Ag-MOFs crystals via a DMFfree green process, respectively. Then CNF as a green crosslinker was added to the mixed system for enhancing mechanical strength of filters. The filtration efficiency, nitrogen adsorption ability and antibacterial performance of filters were investigated. Meanwhile, the contributions of CNF on the improved mechanical strength of filters were also evaluated. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first time to study the effects of double-component MOFs on the gas adsorption and antibacterial ability of cellulose-based air filters. Our work provides a valuable reference for fabricating cellulose-based filters with great filtration performance, adsorption ability, antibacterial activity and mechanical strength, applied in PM2.5 removal and health security.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
Zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , AR) and silver nitrate (AgNO3, AR) were all obtained from Damao Chemical Co., Ltd. (Tianjing, China). 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid ( $\mathrm{\bf~\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{\bfBTC}$ , AR), Imidazole $\mathrm{\C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , AR) and 2-methylimidazole $(\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , AR) were purchased from Aladdin Industrial Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Cellulose nanofiber (CNF) with an average diameter of $10\mathrm{nm}$ and an average length of $2~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ was purchased from Wood Elf Biotechnology Co., Ltd (Tianjing, China). The softwood Kraft pulp board with the solid content of $95.5\%$ was provided by Shan Dong Pulp＆Paper Co., Ltd, China. The pulp was refined to $89~^{\circ}\mathrm{SR}$ using a PFI refiner.
## 2.2. Preparation of Ag-MOFs@CNF@CF air filter
Softwood pulp boards were immersed and diluted with distilled water to form uniform pulp suspension. Then the suspension at $1.5~\mathrm{wt}\%$ was poured into a Petri dish and kept at ${}^{-56}{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . Finally, a pure cellulose filter was obtained after placing it in a BILON freeze-dryer (Shanghai Bilang Instrument Manufacture Co. Ltd, China) at - $.56^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under $10\mathrm{Pa}$ for 3 days. And the preparation of Ag-MOFs@CNF@CF air filter was similar as mentioned above. 2.4 mmol $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ was added into pulp suspension with continuous stirring at room temperature. After 20 minutes, 9.4 mmol AgNO3 and 7.3 mmol imidazole were poured into the reaction system. When mixed suspension became homogeneous and mushy, the suspension was sealed in a $200\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave and heated at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The Ag-MOFs $@$ cellulose fibers were washed with $500\mathrm{mL}$ distilled water to remove the residual reactants. Subsequently, the CNF $\left(0.3506\ \mathrm{g}\right.$ , oven-dried) was added into the Erlenmeyer flask loaded with AgMOFs $@$ cellulose fibers to form homogeneous suspension. And the AgMOFs $@$ CNF@CF filter was obtained after the freeze-drying process as mentioned
above.
## 2.3. Preparation of ZIF-8@CNF@CF air filter
In a typical procedure, 2.6 mmol of $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ was poured into pulp suspension with stirring constantly at room temperature. After 15 minutes, 2- methylimidazole (16.5 mmol) was added into the reaction system. The mixture of pulp suspension was sealed in a $200~\mathrm{mL}$ autoclave to continue the reaction and ensure the integrity of ZIF-8 crystals at $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The ZIF- $\cdot8@$ cellulose fibers were washed with $500\mathrm{mL}$ distilled water to remove the residual reactants. Similarly, the mixture ZIF8@CNF@CF suspension was prepared by adding the same amounts of CNF with stirring constantly. And the ZIF-8 $@$ CNF@CF filter was obtained after the freezedrying process as mentioned above.
## 2.4 Preparation of Ag-MOFs@CNF@ZIF-8 air filter
The preparation of Ag-MOFs@CNF@ZIF-8 filter was exhibited in Fig. 1. The Ag-MOFs $@$ cellulose and ZIF-8@cellulose fibers were firstly synthesized as mentioned above (Fig. 1-1 and Fig. 1-2). Then the same amounts of CNF was added into the Erlenmeyer flask loaded with Ag-MOFs $@$ cellulose fibers to form homogeneous mixed suspension. Subsequently, the suspension at $1.5\mathrm{wt}\%$ was poured into a Petri dish and kept at ${}^{-56}{}^{\circ}{\bf C}$ for $4\mathrm{h}$ . Similarly, the mixture of ZIF-8@CNF@CF suspension was prepared by adding the same amounts of CNF with stirring constantly. Finally, the mixed suspension was transferred into the Petri dish loaded with AgMOFs@CNF cellulose fibers and kept at $-56\mathrm{~~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\mathrm{~4~h~}$ . And the AgMOFs@CNF@ZIF-8 air filter was obtained after the freeze-drying process as mentioned above. Moreover, we prepared the CNF $@$ CF filter as a control sample to further study the effects of double-component MOFs and CNF on antibacterial activity and mechanical properties of filters. Additionally, the distilled water was used as a solvent to dilute pulp suspension and all chemicals in this experiment.
