## 2.8. Antibacterial performance
## 2.8.1. Antibacterial activities of Ag-MOFs and ZIF-8
Escherichia coli $E.$ . coli) was used as a model bacterium to studied antibacterial activities of samples. The $E$ . coli cultures were grown in a Luria-Bertani (LB) broth at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ until log phase by measuring the optical density at $600~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Then the bacterial cells were diluted to $10^{7}\mathrm{\CFU\mL^{-1}}$ colony forming units. Different concentrations (1280, 320, 80, 20, $10~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1},$ of Ag-MOFs and ZIF-8 were put into asepsis tubes including 3 mL LB liquid medium. Subsequently, the $E$ . coli suspension contain $100\upmu\mathrm{L}$ $(10^{5}\mathrm{\CFU\mL^{-1}})$ were poured into asepsis tubes and incubated at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Afterwards, the number of the colonies was carefully counted, and the bacteriostasis rate was calculated based on Eq. (4) (Qian et al., 2018) to evaluate the antibacterial activities of Ag-MOFs and ZIF-8.
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{Bacteriostasis~rate}=\frac{(A-B)}{A}{\times}100\%}\end{array}
$$
where $A$ and $B$ are number of the colonies detected from the blank and MOFs crystals, respectively. Six repetitions were carried out to obtain an average data for each sample.
## 2.8.2. Inhibition zone technique
The antibacterial activities of composite filters were evaluated by the inhibition zone testing (Chauhan et al. 2015). Luria-Bertani (LB) broth medium and all Petri dishes were disinfected at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $15\mathrm{min}$ in an autoclave, respectively. Then the LB medium was uniformly poured into the Petri dishes with a diameter of $60~\mathrm{mm}$ and cooled down to solidification at room temperature. Subsequently, E.coli suspension was evenly spread over the solid medium. The filters samples with a diameter of 6 mm were carefully placed in the center of the dishes and incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . Finally, the dishes were taken photo and the diameter of inhibition zone was measured.
## 2.9. Mechanical property
The stress–strain curves were commonly used to evaluate the mechanical property of filters. The filter samples were compressed with an AI-700-NGD universal material testing apparatus (Taiwan) to measure their mechanical properties. The compression speed of the load cell was set at $2\mathrm{mm}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ and the load cell was about $4900\mathrm{N}$ .
## Results and discussion
## 3.1. Design and preparation of Ag-MOFs@CNF@ZIF-8 filter
The fabrication procedure of the Ag-MOFs $@$ CNF@ZIF-8 filter is shown in Fig. 2. As previous literatures reported (Russell, & Migone, 2017; Lu, Ye, Zhang, et al., 2014), the Ag-MOFs and ZIF-8 crystals both owned gas adsorption properties and antibacterial activities against E. coli. To endow cellulose filters with various performances, fibrillated cellulose fibers were firstly functionalized by Ag-MOFs. The Ag-MOFs would grow on the surface of fibers due to the formation of ester bond association between the hydroxyl groups of cellulose fibers and the carboxyl groups of H3BTC (Yang et al., 2017). ZIF-8 crystal was consisted of Zn atoms interconnected with 2-methylimidazolate ligands via coordinating N atoms in the 1,3-positions of the imidazole ring (Shah, Kwon, Tran, Sachdeva, & Jeong, 2013). The deposition mechanisms of ZIF-8 crystals on the surface of cellulose fibers were mainly based on electrostatic interaction (Su, Zhang, Lu, Song, & Zhao, 2018) and hydrogen interaction (Chauhan et al., 2015). In addition, CNF can serve as a green bridging substance (Lu, Hu, Xie, & Hao, 2017) to fabricate composite filters with multi-layer structure according to strong hydrogen bonding. As shown in Fig. 2, the Ag-MOFs and ZIF-8 crystals were well immobilized on the surface fibers, which would endow cellulose filters with great gas adsorption and antibacterial activity (Qian et al., 2018). Thus, this Ag-MOFs@CNF@ZIF-8 filter could take an overall treatment of polluted air including killing bacteria, PM removal and toxic gas adsorption. Additionally, multilayer structure could be constructed in our composite filters. In this experiment, we chose the double-layers structure as the representative for a thorough study.
3.2. SEM and EDS analyses of Ag-MOFs@CNF@ZIF-8 filter
The results of SEM and EDS showed that Ag-MOFs and ZIF-8 crystals have successfully deposited on the surface of cellulose fibers. As shown in Fig.3 (a-1), (b-1)
(c-1), the middle layer of Ag-MOFs $@$ CNF@ZIF-8 filter displayed smaller and denser pore structure compared with the top and bottom layers. This may be attributed to two aspects: (1) The integration of Ag-MOFs and ZIF-8 crystals would create complex and dense porous structure of composite filters. (2) The abundant presence of CNF would divide large holes into numerous small pores and build a dense spider-web-like structure. As shown in Fig. 3(b-2) and the insert image, the long and thin CNF as bridging substance tightly attached on the surface of fibers and split pores into many parts to form a net structure. Additionally, figure 3(a-2) and (c-2) obviously exhibited that the rod-like crystals (Ag-MOFs) and hexagonal-like crystals (ZIF-8) have immobilized on fibers, respectively. Moreover, the average size of Ag-MOFs crystals were about $2{\cdot}5~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ as shown in Fig. 3(a-3), and the morphology and size of ZIF-8 nanocrystals in Fig. 3(c-3) was similar to previous report by Yao and coworkers (Yao et al., 2013). Meanwhile, the Ag-MOFs and ZIF-8 crystals were also observed in AgMOFs $@$ CNF@ZIF-8 composite filters (Fig. 3(b-3)). And the composite deposition ratio of Ag-MOFs and ZIF-8 crystals on fibers could be up to $76.6\%$ . Furthermore, the element mapping images and spectrum of Ag-MOFs $@$ CNF $@$ ZIF-8 filter were shown in Fig. 4. Three new elements N, Zn and Ag appearing in the composites were observed in Fig. 4(a), suggesting that Ag-MOFs and ZIF-8 crystals had deposited on the surface of fibers by in situ growth method. The weight percentage of $Z\mathrm{n}$ and Ag (Fig. 4(b), 4(c) and 4(d)) were detected as approximately $10\%$ and $18\%$ , respectively, based on the SEM-EDS technique. The above results indicated the double-component MOFs including Ag-MOFs and ZIF-8 have successfully grown on the composite filters.
The detailed XRD, ATR-FTIR and TGA analyses of pure cellulose filter, AgMOFs $@$ CNF@ZIF-8 filter and MOFs crystals were shown in Fig. S1. These analyses are consistent with the results of SEM and EDS, confirming the deposition of MOFs on filters. Furthermore, the results of TGA showed that the thermal stability of AgMOFs $@$ CNF@CF filter was improved compared with pure cellulose filter due to the existence of Ag-MOFs with low thermal conductivities (Qian et al., 2018). In addition, the deposition of MOFs crystals could also endow filters with various functionalities including gas adsorption and antibacterial capacity.
The filtration performances of pure cellulose filter, Ag-MOFs $@$ CNF@CF filter, ZIF8@CNF@CF filter and Ag-MOFs $@$ CNF@ZIF-8 filter were shown in Fig. 5(a). In this experiment, PM2.5 and $\mathbf{PM}_{10}$ as representative polluted particulates were used to test and evaluate the filtration performance of filters. Compared with pure cellulose filter, MOFs@CNF@CF composite filters, especially Ag-MOFs@CNF@ZIF-8 filter showed a higher $\mathrm{PM}_{2.5}$ and $\mathbf{PM}_{10}$ removal efficiency. This may be attributed to two aspects: (1) The presence of double-component MOFs would strengthen the interaction between filters and PM and improve the filtration efficiency of composite filters effectively. Because the defects of ZIF-8 and Ag-MOFs deposited on filters would polarize the surface of PM, causing that polarized PM would easily interact with MOFs with unbalanced metal ions (Zhang et al., 2016; Chen et al., 2017). (2) This special multilayer structure are benefit for intercepting more particles by gradient filtering method, as illustrated in Fig. 5(b). The filters with multi-layer structure would provide a complex tortuous channel for passing particulates, which could increase the frequency of effective collision between filters and particulates (Zhang, Liu, Yin, Yu, & Ding, 2016). Moreover, a key reason for the improvement of filtration performance was the increased specific surface area of filters due to the integration of ZIF-8 nanocrystals (Su, Zhang, Lu, Song, & Zhao, 2018). And this was also the reason that the filtration efficiency of ZIF-8@CNF@CF filter was higher than that of Ag-MOFs $@$ CNF@CF filter. Thus, the Ag-MOFs $@$ CNF@ZIF-8 filter with multi-layer structure shows a great potential in removing PM.
