The nitrogen adsorption results in Fig. 6 exhibit that all samples present Type I adsorption isotherms. The sharply increase in quantity adsorbed from 0.01 to 0.1 (p/p0) can be attributed to the micropore structure of composite filters (Yan, Komarneni, Zhang, & Yan, 2014). It was also observed that the nitrogen adsorption abilities of composite filters, especially Ag-MOFs@CNF@ZIF-8 and Ag-MOFs@CNF@CF filters were higher than that of pure cellulose filter in Fig. 6. The improvement of adsorption capacities was due to the existence of ZIF-8 nanocrystals with abundant adsorption sites and large specific surface area. Large amounts of adsorption sites including organic adsorption sites and open metal sites on the surface of ZIF-8 play a significant role to capture and adsorb gas molecules (Zhang et al., 2016; Barea, Montoro, & Navarro, 2014). Furthermore, the Langmuir specific surface area of ZIF-8 crystals are up to $1545.2\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ as shown in Table Ⅰ. And the integration of ZIF-8 nanocrystals significantly improved the specific surface area of Ag-MOFs $@$ CNF@ZIF-8 filter, which was 126 times higher than pure cellulose filter. The sharply increased specific surface area would be beneficial to improve the gas adsorption capacity of composite filters. In addition, the adsorption process of nitrogen began with the metal center (Zinc) at three adsorption sites. Subsequently, with the pressure increasing, the gas would spread to the organic ligands until all cavities of ZIF-8 crystals were filled (Su, Zhang, Lu, Song, & Zhao, 2018). In previous literatures, Russell et al. reported that ZIF-8 crystals owned excellent gas adsorption ability for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ (Russell & Migone, 2017). Zhang et al. prepared metal−organic framework filters with a selectively capture and adsorption capacity for $\mathrm{SO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ mixture (Zhang et al., 2016). The results indicated that Ag-MOFs $@$ CNF@ZIF-8 air filter owned excellent gas adsorption capacity. And the filter may be potentially used as a promising and versatile air filter to adsorb toxic gases and purify air based on above literatures.
3.5. Antibacterial performance analyses of ZIF-8, Ag-MOFs and Ag-MOFs@CNF@ ZIF-8 filter
In this study, MOFs crystals were employed to endow cellulose filters with various functionalities such as gas adsorption, antibacterial ability. Thus, the antibacterial activities of Ag-MOFs and ZIF-8 were investigated by the spread plate method (Qian et al., 2018). And the bacteriostasis rate is shown in Fig. 7. Both Ag-MOFs and ZIF-8 crystals exhibited significant antibacterial properties. When the Ag-MOFs and ZIF-8 concentration was set at $10~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}\varTheta\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , the bacteriostasis rate of Ag-MOFs and ZIF-8 could reach $86.9\%$ and $65.2\%$ , respectively. Compared with ZIF-8, Ag-MOFs crystals showed superior ability of inhibiting bacterial proliferation. In addition, the antibacterial mechanism of MOFs crystals would be attributed to the release of metal ions and organic ligands. The active metal ions such as $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , ${\mathbf{A}}{\mathbf{g}}^{+}$ could interact with hydroxyl group of peptidoglycan membrane and oxidize membrane proteins and fatty acids (Wahab, Mishra, Yun, Kim, & Shin, 2010), resulting in the breakage of cell membrane and cell death (Feng et al., 2000; Wang, Qian, & An, 2015). Meanwhile, functional groups of organic ligands in MOFs would bond with $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ etc. in cell, causing fragmentation of DNA and rupture of cell membrane (Lu, Ye, Zhang, et al., 2014; Aguado et al., 2014).
The antibacterial performances of filter samples were evaluated by the inhibition zone test according to previous reports (Chauhan et al., 2015). As shown in Fig.8(a), $E.$ . coli grew well on the surface of the dish with CNF $@$ CF filter, indicating that pristine CNF $@$ CF filter had no antibacterial activity against E. coli. On the contrary, clear inhibition zones on the dishes with MOFs $@$ CNF $@$ CF composites were observed in Fig. 8(b), (c), (d). Furthermore, the inhibition zone in the dish with Ag-MOFs@CNF@CF filter ( $13.2~\mathrm{mm}$ ) was larger than that ZIF-8@CNF@CF composites $\mathrm{9.8~mm}\mathrm{,}$ . This could be attributed to that the antibacterial activity of Ag-MOFs crystals was obviously superior than ZIF-8 as mentioned above. And the Ag-MOFs@CNF@ZIF-8 filter exhibited excellent antibacterial performances with an inhibition zone diameter of 18.1 mm due to the deposition of double-component MOFs crystals. Therefore, the above results indicated that Ag-MOFs $@$ CNF@ZIF-8 filter has a great potential in killing bacteria as a functional air filter.
Compression performance as a representative and key parameter was commonly used to evaluate the mechanical property of filters. The dynamic compression stress– strain curves of filters were shown in Fig. 9(a). The compressive strength of CNF@CF filters was significantly improved and could reach $485\mathrm{{kPa}}$ , which was approximately 3 times higher than that of pure cellulose filter. This can be attributed to the enhanced lamellar structure as shown in Fig. 9(b) and (c). The CNF was used as bridge substance to reinforce lamellar structure by pivotal hydrogen bonding (Svagan et al., 2008; Sehaqui et al., 2011). The strong hydrogen bonding and physical entanglement effect of CNF with cellulose fibers significantly improved the interfacial bonding between cellulosic layers in Fig. 9(d) (Tabarsa, Sheykhnazari, Ashori, Mashkour, & Khazaeian, 2017). Furthermore, the enhanced effects of bridging-link between cellulosic layers could prevent Euler buckling of lamellar porous structure and resist higher compression strength (Deville, 2008). Compared with CNF $@$ CF filter, Ag-MOFs $@$ CNF@ZIF-8 filter exhibited a higher compressive strength due to the enhancing of doublecomponent MOFs. The integration of MOFs crystals into composite filters would provide abundant active sites to improve the interaction of cellulosic molecular chains and enhance the strength of porous structure (Qian et al., 2018). Overall, the compression strength of Ag-MOFs $@$ CNF@ZIF-8 filter is suitable for removing PM2.5 and healthcare fields as a multifunctional air filter.
## Conclusions
In summary, a novel and biodegradable Ag-MOFs $@$ CNF@ZIF-8 filter with multilayer structure was fabricated via in situ generation process. And the filter exhibits excellent filtration performance, gas adsorption, high antibacterial activity and mechanical property. The presence of MOFs crystals would enhance the interaction between filter and PM, significantly improving the filtration efficiency of filters. Particularly, the integration of ZIF-8 nanocrystals greatly improved the specific surface area of filters and endowed them with high gas adsorption capacity due to abundant adsorption sites. In addition, due to the release of active metal ions including $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{+}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ from MOFs crystals and the strong interfacial bonding of CNF between cellulosic layers, the Ag-MOFs $@$ CNF@ZIF-8 filter not only showed high antibacterial activity against E. coli, but also exhibited excellent mechanical performance. Thus, this multifunctional cellulose-based air filter could be a promising candidate in removing PM2.5, adsorbing toxic gases and health security fields.
